From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] d3d9umd: DXVA2 decoding, on the Direct3D 11 driver's DXVA decoding

Players with DXVA2 hardware decoding (MPC-HC/BE, Kodi, VLC's dxva2) and
ffmpeg's -hwaccel dxva2 got no decoder: the D3D9 driver's Decode* entry
points were stubs and its caps listed none.

- d3d10umd: the DXVA decoding (DXVA's structures, now Dxva*-prefixed: the
  D3D9 side's WDK headers define some of the names; the profiles; the
  slices; the frame's buffers to a gallium picture description) moves
  from Video.cpp into DxvaDecode.{h,cpp}, which both drivers build. The
  frontend brings the pictures: an output texture's array slice in
  Direct3D 11, unchanged.
- d3d9umd/decode.cpp: the decoder caps (the NV12 profiles: H.264, HEVC
  Main, VP9 profile 0, AV1, MPEG-2), the compressed buffers (memory of
  the driver's, through locks), and the Decode* entry points. Each NV12
  surface the decoder writes gets the codec's video buffer (R8/R8G8
  planes, the host's reference too); after each frame a draw on the
  video context copies them into the surface's L8 texture (luma rows,
  then chroma rows), which locks, StretchRect and the video processor
  read. DXVA's surface indices name the surface last decoded with that
  index. The two contexts' work is finished before the other uses it.

ffmpeg -hwaccel dxva2: H.264 (field and MBAFF conformance clips too),
HEVC up to 4K, VP9, AV1 with film grain, all bit-exact with software;
10-bit streams fall back to software (no P010 surfaces in D3D9).
---
diff --git a/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/DxvaDecode.cpp b/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/DxvaDecode.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4b86b12
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/gallium/frontends/d3d10umd/DxvaDecode.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,775 @@
